  "query_preview": "..The upper level low that moved into eastern AZ\nTuesday will continue northward reaching southern UT/CO by this\nafternoon. Otherwise, deep moisture will remain over the region\ntoday with another round of afternoon/evening showers and\nthunderstorms. Storm motion will be slower today for most of the\narea, with a bit higher motion toward the north along the NM line\nto the east of the low. This slow storm motion will bring a\ncontinued threat of locally heavy rainfall and flash flooding with\nstorms, though the risk is a bit lower than previous days. By\nThursday, the ridge to our east will start to shift toward the\nstate. Although monsoon moisture will remain, precipitable water\nvalues won't be quite as high as we have seen over the past week,\nand temperatures aloft will start to warm. These factors will lead\nto less coverage of shower and thunderstorm activity, and becoming\nmore focused on the higher terrain. Light southerly steering flow\nwill also keep Yavapai and northern Gila pretty quiet without the\npush of storms off the rim country that we have seen for many days\nin a row. This trend continues Friday and Saturday. The ridge\nbecomes rather strong over Arizona as well and this will lead to\nmuch warmer temperatures. We're looking at highs 5-10 degrees\nabove normal by Friday and the weekend, and a heat warning product\nmay be needed for the lower elevations of Grand Canyon, with\ngenerally moderate HeatRisk elsewhere. By early next week, most of\nthe guidance shifts the ridge back to the east, allowing for a\nreturn of somewhat higher precipitable water values and cooler\ntemperatures aloft. Storm chances recover back to the 30-50% range\nfor much of the area.",
